@@ -0,0 +1,96 @@
+---
+name: perf-investigate
+description: Read the bench job's instruction counts, find what made a benchmark slower, and choose between a fix and a performance ADR for the owner. Use when the bench step fails with [E-BENCH], is pending, prints [W-BENCH-EXCUSED], or before a change meant to cost instructions.
+---
+
+# Benchmarks: reading a regression
+
+The bench job (spec 6.13; rung 2 plan, decision 14) counts instructions
+with gungraun under valgrind's callgrind (its `Ir`) for the benchmarks in
+`crates/eng-bench/benches/`: the dot and room ticks, the room's build,
+grid traces and `ground_move`. It builds and runs the pushed head and the
+push's base on one Linux x64 runner. There is no stored baseline. Counts
+are not times: the same code gives the same count on any Linux x64
+machine, so a change in a count is the code's, not the runner's.
+
+- **Fails** (`[E-BENCH]`): a head count more than 5% above the base's
+  (`head * 100 > base * 105`, in integers), or a benchmark the base has
+  and the head lacks, unless the push adds an accepted performance ADR
+  that names it.
+- **Pending** (not a pass, fails no job): off Linux, without valgrind or
+  `gungraun-runner` 0.20.0 (it must equal eng-bench's gungraun), or a base
+  equal to the head. On CI a missing tool fails instead.
+- **Passes:** every count within 5%, and any benchmark new in the head.
+
+## Read the result
+
+- **The base**, in the step's output: on `main`, the push's `before`
+  (`ENG_BENCH_BASE`) if it is an ancestor; off `main`, the merge base with
+  `origin/main`; or `--base REV`. On an agent branch that is the merge
+  base, not your previous push, so an earlier push's regression still
+  shows.
+- **The report**: CI's `report-bench` artifact holds
+  `target/ci/reports/bench-<host>.json`; fetch it as the `golden-update`
+  skill fetches artifacts. Its `bench` key has both commits, every
+  benchmark's `name`, `head`, `base`, `change_percent` and `verdict`, and
+  the ADRs the push added, with whether each excuses. `logs-bench` has
+  each step's log, `cargo bench` for both trees among them.
+- **Names** are gungraun ids, `<bench>::<group>::<function>::<id>`, as
+  `sim::tick::room::walk`. The bench is `crates/eng-bench/benches/sim/` or
+  `crates/eng-bench/benches/collision/`; its `cases.rs` builds each input
+  and makes the one call a benchmark measures.
+
+## Reproduce
+
+gungraun runs only on Linux (elsewhere each bench is an empty `main`).
+On another OS use a Linux x64 container with valgrind and the runner:
+`cargo install --locked gungraun-runner@0.20.0`, then
+`cargo xtask bench --base <rev>` in the checkout. It measures the working
+tree as the head, in `target/bench/head`, and the base in a detached
+worktree at `target/bench/tree`, built into `target/bench/base`, then
+removes the worktree. One bench alone: `cargo bench -p eng-bench --bench sim`.
+`crates/eng-bench/tests/cases.rs` runs every case natively on any target,
+which checks the inputs but measures nothing.
+
+## Find the cause
+
+1. **Which benchmarks moved, and together?** One alone points at the call
+   it measures. Several together point at code they share: every room
+   tick runs `ground_move`, whose ground probe and moves are grid traces.
+2. **What changed under them:**
+   `git diff <base> HEAD -- crates/eng-collision-grid crates/kit-fps crates/game-testbed-sim crates/eng-sim crates/eng-ecs`.
+3. **Usual causes:** an extra trace per tick (a planted second ground probe
+   cost 12% to 18% on three benchmarks when the gate was built); a `Vec`
+   built inside a hot loop; more candidates per trace (a larger swept box,
+   a cell visit that grew); work moved before an early exit; a check that
+   runs every tick but could run once at build (sim data is built once).
+   Release overflow checks are on for the core set, in head and base alike.
+4. **Where the instructions went:** gungraun keeps callgrind's output files
+   under each tree's target directory; `callgrind_annotate` on the head's
+   and the base's files for one benchmark shows the functions that grew.
+5. **Did the case change?** A benchmark compares like with like only if its
+   input in `cases.rs` is the same in both trees. `tests/cases.rs` checks
+   each input still does what its name says. A push that slows the code
+   and shrinks a case together hides a regression, which is why the
+   benches are protected from G5.
+
+## Decide
+
+- **Fix it** when the cost buys nothing: bring the count back within 5%,
+  push again, and the step passes.
+- **Propose a performance ADR** when the cost is worth paying (correctness,
+  or a feature the benchmark now exercises). Add a new
+  `docs/adr/<NNNN>-<title>.md` in the same push, with its status
+  `Proposed`, naming each benchmark it excuses in backticks
+  (`` `sim::tick::room::walk` ``), the base and head counts, why the cost is
+  needed and what would win it back; add its row to the ADR index. Only
+  the owner marks it Accepted. Off `main` the excuse is read from
+  `origin/main`'s copy of the file, so until the owner accepts it there
+  the step stays pending (`[W-BENCH-EXCUSED] … once the owner accepts`),
+  never passing. An ADR must be new in the push to excuse anything;
+  editing an accepted one to add a name excuses nothing.
+- **Never** shrink a case, drop or rename a benchmark (a benchmark gone
+  fails as a regression does), change gungraun's settings, or mark an ADR
+  Accepted yourself.
+- Put the numbers (base, head, percent) and the cause in the commit
+  message and the rung's `PROGRESS.md` entry.
